Survey Results for Moller Polarimeter Target Holder
Performed Nov 5th and 6th by Ethan Becker and William Henry

November 5th

TEST 1
(4.93 V source for rotary pot from LT1027 regulator with 9.03 V battery) 
We started by installing the target holder in the magnet.  This was done to determine the position of the target holder fiducials with respect to the magnet’s fiducials.  While on the magnet we measured the range of motion of the flange with the screws partially in.  This was 2mm(back and forth?).  This means when we install the target holder we need to rotate or push the flange in a predetermined direction each time we install in the magnet to preserve alignment.  Rotate Clockwise looking down!!

The target holder was then moved to the test stand for the rest of the surveying.  

TEST 2
The next test was performed to study the flatness and reproducibility of the beam line direction position of the target ladder.  The ladder was scanned at the fully retracted position (3.520 volts)  and extended position (4.931 volts).  
(Rotary pot.->1.866 Volts).  The average deviation at the four corners of the ladder was 80 µm(upstream?).  Note- 80µm in 2" corresponds to .09 degrees.

NOVEMBER 8th

TEST 3
5 V source for rotary pot. from maxnet card)
Next test was to remove the ladder(to install foils later) and then replace it to see how the angle with respect to the beam line changed.  The shaft has a flat are ground down where two cup set screws secure the ladder.  The rotary voltage before was 3.0315.  After the ladder was put back the voltage read 3.0277.  Survey said the ladder moved .87 degrees.  We rotated the foil back to 3.0311V.  Survey then said the ladder was .55 degrees off from the beginning position.

TEST 4
Rotation Tests
See Excel File
Despite a not so reliable voltage source and a limited voltage range we were able to show control of the ladder rotation within .2 degrees.  Repeatability was within 1/10 of a degree.

TEST 5
Translation Test
The center of the foils is 1.5mm to the left of beam line.
The limit switch centers are 1.3 mm off, need to adjust.
No noticible backlash(only one measurement to check this)
Repeatability within .2 mm (two measurements) 

TEST 6
Magnetic Coupling
The largest error was when we bumped/shook the ladder.
The ladder was initially at 89.7°.
Then we bumped it and it was at 89.3°.
Another bump and it was at 89.8°.
This gives a .5° uncertainity.
When it is time for the final survey before installation in the beam line we should perform this test again but then move the ladder to the negative limit and back to the starting position to see how this effects the angle.  
